          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with lobar pneumonia begins producing rusty-coloured sputum on day 3 o</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with lobar pneumonia begins producing rusty-coloured sputum on day 3 of illness. Which pathological stage best explains this finding?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Congestion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Congestion is the earliest stage, characterised by serous fluid leaking from engorged capillaries. Rusty sputum has not yet developed at this point.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Red hepatisation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In red hepatisation the alveoli fill with intact red blood cells, fibrin, and neutrophils, making the lobe firm and dark red. Rusty discolouration requires lysis of those RBCs, which occurs in the next stage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Grey hepatisation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In grey hepatisation, trapped red blood cells lyse and their degradation products mix with the exudate, producing the characteristic rusty-brown sputum.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Resolution</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In resolution, proteolytic enzymes digest the fibrin exudate and macrophages clear debris. Sputum production declines as normal alveolar architecture is restored.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the four stages of lobar pneumonia. Rusty sputum arises during grey hepatisation, when trapped red blood cells lyse and their breakdown products discolour the exudate."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Grey hepatisation is the stage where RBC lysis produces the rusty-brown exudate that the patient coughs up as rusty sputum — a classic clinical clue for lobar pneumonia."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
