          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 50-year-old quarry worker has bilateral upper-lobe nodules and eggshell calcif</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 50-year-old quarry worker has bilateral upper-lobe nodules and eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes. He develops active tuberculosis. Which mechanism best explains his increased susceptibility?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Silica particles directly lyse mycobacterial cell walls</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Silica has no direct antimicrobial effect. Its pathogenicity lies in its interaction with alveolar macrophages, not with the bacilli themselves.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Calcium deposits in hilar nodes compress bronchi, trapping bacilli</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Eggshell calcification is a radiological hallmark of silicosis but does not mechanistically explain TB susceptibility. The mechanism operates at the cellular level, not the anatomical level.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Silica impairs macrophage bactericidal function, reducing mycobacterial killing</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Silica uniquely disables the bactericidal function of alveolar macrophages, allowing ingested M. tuberculosis to survive and proliferate intracellularly. This makes silicotuberculosis a recognised and serious combination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fibrotic nodules create hypoxic zones where anaerobic bacilli thrive</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>M. tuberculosis is an obligate aerobe, not an anaerobe — it actually favours the well-oxygenated lung apices. The mechanism of silicotuberculosis is macrophage dysfunction, not tissue hypoxia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key concept is that silica uniquely impairs the bactericidal function of alveolar macrophages. This allows M. tuberculosis to survive and replicate inside the very cells meant to destroy it, making silicotuberculosis a dangerous combination."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Silica disables macrophage killing, allowing TB bacilli to survive intracellularly — the cellular basis of silicotuberculosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
