          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 65-year-old retired insulation worker presents with a unilateral pleural effus</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 65-year-old retired insulation worker presents with a unilateral pleural effusion. Biopsy shows a malignant epithelioid neoplasm that is calretinin-positive, WT1-positive, and TTF-1-negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Metastatic lung adenocarcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Metastatic adenocarcinoma would typically show TTF-1 and CEA positivity with calretinin negativity — the reverse of the pattern described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Malignant mesothelioma</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Calretinin, WT1, D2-40, and CK5/6 positivity with TTF-1 and CEA negativity identifies mesothelioma. The 25–45-year latency from asbestos exposure fits the occupational history of a retired insulation worker.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Squamous cell carcinoma is typically a central lung mass, not a primary pleural tumour, and would not show calretinin or WT1 positivity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Small-cell lung carcinoma with pleural metastasis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Small-cell carcinoma is always central, shows neuroendocrine markers, and would not express calretinin or WT1. Pleural metastases from any lung primary would show a different IHC profile.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the IHC panel: calretinin, WT1, D2-40, and CK5/6 positivity identifies mesothelioma. TTF-1, CEA, and MOC-31 positivity points to metastatic adenocarcinoma instead. The occupational history of asbestos exposure is a crucial clinical clue."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The calretinin/WT1-positive, TTF-1-negative panel is the diagnostic fingerprint for mesothelioma, and the occupational exposure to asbestos with decades of latency completes the clinical picture."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
